[[:db/cas 17592186272202 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{′}(x)=-5x$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=-3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186272202 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-3{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-6x \\\\  & \\ne -5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-3{x}^{2}$ and ${y}^{′}=-6x$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -6x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2(-3{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\ -6x & =-6x\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-3{x}^{2}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement ${f}^{′}(x)=-5x$ is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-3{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-6x \\\\  & \\ne -5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is true. Substituting $y=-3{x}^{2}$ and ${y}^{′}=-6x$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -6x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2(-3{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\ -6x & =-6x\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation” is true. Since statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is true, the function $y=-3{x}^{2}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement”; “The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation” only"] [:db/cas 17592186439013 :answer-field/key "I" "${f}^{′}(x)=-5x$"] [:db/cas 17592186439014 :answer-field/key "II" "Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement"] [:db/cas 17592186439015 :answer-field/key "III" "The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation"] [:db/cas 17592186054945 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are finite?\n- **I.** $P(N)$\n- **II.** $P({Z,\\pi })$\n- **III.** $P({x\\in Z∣\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0})$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following sets are finite?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186054945 :question/worked-solution "The power set of a finite set is finite, while the power set of an infinite set is infinite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Set I is infinite since the underlying set $N$ is infinite.\n\n- Set II is finite since the underlying set is finite. Notice that \n\n$$\nP({Z,\\pi })={∅,\\,{Z},\\,{\\pi },\\,{Z,\\pi }}\n$$\n\nhas cardinality $4.$\n\n- Set III is finite since the underlying set is finite. Notice that \n\n$$\n{x\\in Z∣\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0}={0},\n$$\n\nand, as a result, \n\n$$\nP({x\\in Z∣\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0})=P({0})={∅,\\,{0}}\n$$\n\nhas cardinality $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "The power set of a finite set is finite, while the power set of an infinite set is infinite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Set $P(N)$ is infinite since the underlying set $N$ is infinite.\n\n- Set $P({Z,\\pi })$ is finite since the underlying set is finite. Notice that \n\n$$\nP({Z,\\pi })={∅,\\,{Z},\\,{\\pi },\\,{Z,\\pi }}\n$$\n\nhas cardinality $4.$\n\n- Set $P({x\\in Z∣\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0})$ is finite since the underlying set is finite. Notice that \n\n$$\n{x\\in Z∣\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0}={0},\n$$\n\nand, as a result, \n\n$$\nP({x\\in Z∣\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0})=P({0})={∅,\\,{0}}\n$$\n\nhas cardinality $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $P({Z,\\pi })$; $P({x\\in Z∣\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0})$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186477515 :answer-field/key "I" "$P(N)$"] [:db/cas 17592186477516 :answer-field/key "II" "$P({Z,\\pi })$"] [:db/cas 17592186477517 :answer-field/key "III" "$P({x\\in Z∣\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0})$"] [:db/cas 17592186054987 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in Z:-2\\le x<6}$ and $B={x\\in N:x+1<6}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $∅⊆B$\n- **II.** $B⊆A$\n- **III.** $A⊈B$" "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in Z:-2\\le x<6}$ and $B={x\\in N:x+1<6}?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186054987 :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all integer numbers greater than or equal to $-2$ but less than $6.$ So,\n\n$$\nA={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $x+1<6.$ So,\n\n$$\nB={1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $∅⊆B$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $-1\\in A$ but $-1\\notin B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all integer numbers greater than or equal to $-2$ but less than $6.$ So,\n\n$$\nA={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $x+1<6.$ So,\n\n$$\nB={1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $∅⊆B$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $-1\\in A$ but $-1\\notin B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $∅⊆B$; $B⊆A$; $A⊈B$."] [:db/cas 17592186449088 :answer-field/key "I" "$∅⊆B$"] [:db/cas 17592186449089 :answer-field/key "II" "$B⊆A$"] [:db/cas 17592186449090 :answer-field/key "III" "$A⊈B$"] [:db/cas 17592186054993 :question/problem "For the sets $A={2,4,6},$ $B={x,y},$ and $C=A\\times B,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(2,4)\\in C$\n- **II.** $(2,x)\\in C$\n- **III.** $(3,y)\\in C$" "For the sets $A={2,4,6},$ $B={x,y},$ and $C=A\\times B,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186054993 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $A\\times B$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/df/df4277feac4c4e838538f941aa0a07d087beb9005d8f7a8347dadc1da7fe975e.png)\n\nHence, the set $C=A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nC={(2,x),(2,y),(4,x),(4,y),(6,x),(6,y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $A\\times B$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/df/df4277feac4c4e838538f941aa0a07d087beb9005d8f7a8347dadc1da7fe975e.png)\n\nHence, the set $C=A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nC={(2,x),(2,y),(4,x),(4,y),(6,x),(6,y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(2,x)\\in C$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186449143 :answer-field/key "I" "$(2,4)\\in C$"] [:db/cas 17592186449144 :answer-field/key "II" "$(2,x)\\in C$"] [:db/cas 17592186449145 :answer-field/key "III" "$(3,y)\\in C$"] [:db/cas 17592186054995 :question/problem "For the sets $Z=\\{-1,0,1\\}$ and $Y=Z\\times Z,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(-1,0,1)\\in Y$\n\n- **II.** $(-1,1)\\in Y$\n\n- **III.** $(0,0)\\in Y$" "For the sets $Z=\\{-1,0,1\\}$ and $Y=Z\\times Z,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186054995 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $Z\\times Z$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $Z$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Z.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for ${Z}^{2}=Z\\times Z$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6cb962e74701db361807c237a521f13a1c753e4acc6d68a1cfb1694bdfbd45bc.png)\n\nHence, the set $Y=Z\\times Z$ is given by\n\n$$\nY={(-1,-1),(-1,0),(-1,1),(0,-1),(0,0),(0,1),(1,-1),(1,0),(1,1)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "The Cartesian product $Z\\times Z$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $Z$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Z.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for ${Z}^{2}=Z\\times Z$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6cb962e74701db361807c237a521f13a1c753e4acc6d68a1cfb1694bdfbd45bc.png)\n\nHence, the set $Y=Z\\times Z$ is given by\n\n$$\nY={(-1,-1),(-1,0),(-1,1),(0,-1),(0,0),(0,1),(1,-1),(1,0),(1,1)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(-1,1)\\in Y$; $(0,0)\\in Y$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186449167 :answer-field/key "I" "$(-1,0,1)\\in Y$"] [:db/cas 17592186449168 :answer-field/key "II" "$(-1,1)\\in Y$"] [:db/cas 17592186449169 :answer-field/key "III" "$(0,0)\\in Y$"] [:db/cas 17592186055034 :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical statements?\n- **P.** $P:2\\,|\\,4$\n- **Q.** $Q:\\sqrt{2}\\in N$\n- **R.** $R:2+2$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following are mathematical statements?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186460669 :answer-field/key "P" "$P:2\\,|\\,4$"] [:db/cas 17592186460670 :answer-field/key "Q" "$Q:\\sqrt{2}\\in N$"] [:db/cas 17592186460671 :answer-field/key "R" "$R:2+2$"] [:db/cas 17592186055035 :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical statements?\n- **P.** $P:{1,2,3}\\cap {4,5,6}=∅$\n- **Q.** $Q:\\sqrt{{3}^{2}-{4}^{2}}$\n- **R.** $R:4$ is a prime number.\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following are mathematical statements?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186460672 :answer-field/key "P" "$P:{1,2,3}\\cap {4,5,6}=∅$"] [:db/cas 17592186460673 :answer-field/key "Q" "$Q:\\sqrt{{3}^{2}-{4}^{2}}$"] [:db/cas 17592186460674 :answer-field/key "R" "$R:4$ is a prime number."] [:db/cas 17592186055044 :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical predicates of one variable?\n- **I.** $P(n):$ $n$ is even.\n- **II.** $Q(x):$ ${x}^{2}-x\\ge 1$\n- **III.** $R(y):$ Draw a line of width $y\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following are mathematical predicates of one variable?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186055044 :question/worked-solution "A *predicate* of one variable is a sentence that contains the variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute any concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $n$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $x$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $y$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false. Rather, it will give an instruction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "A *predicate* of one variable is a sentence that contains the variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute any concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option “$P(n):$ $n$ is even” is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $n$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option “$Q(x):$ ${x}^{2}-x\\ge 1$” is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $x$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option “$R(y):$ Draw a line of width $y\\,\\text{cm}.$” is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $y$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false. Rather, it will give an instruction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$P(n):$ $n$ is even”; “$Q(x):$ ${x}^{2}-x\\ge 1$” only"] [:db/cas 17592186460675 :answer-field/key "I" "$P(n):$ $n$ is even."] [:db/cas 17592186460676 :answer-field/key "II" "$Q(x):$ ${x}^{2}-x\\ge 1$"] [:db/cas 17592186460677 :answer-field/key "III" "$R(y):$ Draw a line of width $y\\,\\text{cm}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186055071 :question/problem "If $A=\\{0,1,2\\}$ and $B=\\{x,y\\},$ which of the following elements belong to $(A\\times B)\\times A?$\n\nI. $(1,y,2)$\n\nII. $((1,y),2)$\n\nIII. $((y,1),y)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "If $A=\\{0,1,2\\}$ and $B=\\{x,y\\},$ which of the following elements belong to $(A\\times B)\\times A?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186055071 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${0,1,2}$ with ${x,y},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/07/077da578323a686ecf0e07cf0a56030fa4ce128a74d408af95b8a3027ad13f28.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(0,x),(0,y),(1,x),(1,y),(2,x),(2,y)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|=3\\cdot 2=6.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(A\\times B)\\times A\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A\\times B$ and $A,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/3a/3a14abffc755dcfccce2d410ea18fbcae42d4afe2fbeffd81e1a291bc9d4968e.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(A\\times B)\\times A|=|A\\times B|\\cdot |A|=6\\cdot 3=18.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $((1,y),2)\\in (A\\times B)\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${0,1,2}$ with ${x,y},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/07/077da578323a686ecf0e07cf0a56030fa4ce128a74d408af95b8a3027ad13f28.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(0,x),(0,y),(1,x),(1,y),(2,x),(2,y)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|=3\\cdot 2=6.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(A\\times B)\\times A\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A\\times B$ and $A,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/3a/3a14abffc755dcfccce2d410ea18fbcae42d4afe2fbeffd81e1a291bc9d4968e.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(A\\times B)\\times A|=|A\\times B|\\cdot |A|=6\\cdot 3=18.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $((1,y),2)\\in (A\\times B)\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $((1,y),2)$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186460699 :answer-field/key "I" "$(1,y,2)$"] [:db/cas 17592186460700 :answer-field/key "II" "$((1,y),2)$"] [:db/cas 17592186460701 :answer-field/key "III" "$((y,1),y)$"] [:db/cas 17592186055072 :question/problem "If $A=\\{-1,1\\}$ and $B=\\{u,v\\},$ which of the following elements belong to $B\\times B\\times A?$\n\nI. $(v,v,1)$\n\nII. $((v,v),1)$\n\nIII. $(1,v,u)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "If $A=\\{-1,1\\}$ and $B=\\{u,v\\},$ which of the following elements belong to $B\\times B\\times A?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186055072 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ consists of all ordered triples whose first coordinate belongs to $A,$ second coordinate belongs to $B,$ and third coordinate belongs to $C.$\n\nIn this case, $B\\times B\\times A$ consists of every combination of ${u,v}$ with ${u,v}$ and ${-1,1},$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B\\times B\\times A={ & (u,u,-1),(u,u,1),(u,v,-1),(u,v,1), \\\\  & (v,u,-1),(v,u,1),(v,v,-1),(v,v,1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n|B\\times B\\times A|=|B|\\cdot |B|\\cdot |A|=2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $(v,v,1)\\in B\\times B\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "The Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ consists of all ordered triples whose first coordinate belongs to $A,$ second coordinate belongs to $B,$ and third coordinate belongs to $C.$\n\nIn this case, $B\\times B\\times A$ consists of every combination of ${u,v}$ with ${u,v}$ and ${-1,1},$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B\\times B\\times A={ & (u,u,-1),(u,u,1),(u,v,-1),(u,v,1), \\\\  & (v,u,-1),(v,u,1),(v,v,-1),(v,v,1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n|B\\times B\\times A|=|B|\\cdot |B|\\cdot |A|=2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $(v,v,1)\\in B\\times B\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(v,v,1)$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186460702 :answer-field/key "I" "$(v,v,1)$"] [:db/cas 17592186460703 :answer-field/key "II" "$((v,v),1)$"] [:db/cas 17592186460704 :answer-field/key "III" "$(1,v,u)$"] [:db/cas 17592186055242 :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={{1},{2,3},{1,3,6}}?$\n- **I.** ${2,{1}}$\n- **II.** ${{2,3}}$\n- **III.** ${{-1},{1,3,6}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={{1},{2,3},{1,3,6}}?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186055242 :question/worked-solution "A set is a subset of $A$ if all its elements are elements of $A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- ${2,{1}}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element $2\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\n- ${{2,3}}$ is a subset of $A.\\,✓$\n\n- ${{-1},{1,3,6}}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element ${-1}\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "A set is a subset of $A$ if all its elements are elements of $A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- ${2,{1}}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element $2\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\n- ${{2,3}}$ is a subset of $A.\\,✓$\n\n- ${{-1},{1,3,6}}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element ${-1}\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${{2,3}}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186460811 :answer-field/key "I" "${2,{1}}$"] [:db/cas 17592186460812 :answer-field/key "II" "${{2,3}}$"] [:db/cas 17592186460813 :answer-field/key "III" "${{-1},{1,3,6}}$"] [:db/cas 17592186055244 :question/problem "Which of the numbers below belong to ${Q}^{-}?$\n- **I.** $1$\n- **II.** $-\\sqrt{18}$\n- **III.** $\\sqrt{6}$\n- **IV.** $0$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Which of the numbers below belong to ${Q}^{-}?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186460814 :answer-field/key "I" "The number $1$"] [:db/cas 17592186460815 :answer-field/key "II" "$-\\sqrt{18}$"] [:db/cas 17592186460816 :answer-field/key "III" "$\\sqrt{6}$"] [:db/cas 17592186460817 :answer-field/key "IV" "The number $0$"] [:db/cas 17592186055368 :question/problem "![](images/4c/4ce176ba6bb73da8d6af410bedad4733cf3e705c70e28f133d771b89f78130c5.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y = x + \\frac{1}{2}$\n- **II.** $y = \\frac{1}{2}x + 2$\n- **III.** $y = \\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}$" "![](images/4c/4ce176ba6bb73da8d6af410bedad4733cf3e705c70e28f133d771b89f78130c5.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186055368 :question/worked-solution "The line crosses the y-axis at 2 and passes through (4,4); its slope is 1/2. Therefore y=x/2+2, statement II." "The line crosses the y-axis at 2 and passes through (4,4); its slope is 1/2. Therefore y=x/2+2, statement $y = \\frac{1}{2}x + 2$."] [:db/cas 17592186440280 :answer-field/key "I" "$y = x + \\frac{1}{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186440281 :answer-field/key "II" "$y = \\frac{1}{2}x + 2$"] [:db/cas 17592186440282 :answer-field/key "III" "$y = \\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186055390 :question/problem "Which of the following is a variable?\n\nI. $h$\n\nII. $k-2$\n\nIII. $3$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following is a variable?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186055390 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each one in turn.\n\nI. $h$ is a variable because it's a single letter by itself.\n\nII. $k-2$ is an algebraic expression, not a variable.\n\nIII. $3$ is a number, not a variable.\n\nSo, only choice I represents a variable." "Let's look at each one in turn.\n\n“The expression $h$”. $h$ is a variable because it's a single letter by itself.\n\n“The expression $k-2$”. $k-2$ is an algebraic expression, not a variable.\n\n“The expression $3$”. $3$ is a number, not a variable.\n\nSo, only choice “The expression $h$” represents a variable."] [:db/cas 17592186460917 :answer-field/key "I" "The expression $h$"] [:db/cas 17592186460918 :answer-field/key "II" "The expression $k-2$"] [:db/cas 17592186460919 :answer-field/key "III" "The expression $3$"] [:db/cas 17592186055431 :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d78eaaedd26a27d9163fdcddfce5c5ed429e441a75d361f0840935fce9235c6.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x + 2y = 4$\n- **II.** $y = 2 - x$\n- **III.** $y = 4 - x$" "![](images/4d/4d78eaaedd26a27d9163fdcddfce5c5ed429e441a75d361f0840935fce9235c6.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186055431 :question/worked-solution "The line has intercepts (0,4) and (4,0), so its equation is y=4-x, statement III." "The line has intercepts (0,4) and (4,0), so its equation is y=4-x, statement $y = 4 - x$."] [:db/cas 17592186440283 :answer-field/key "I" "$x + 2y = 4$"] [:db/cas 17592186440284 :answer-field/key "II" "$y = 2 - x$"] [:db/cas 17592186440285 :answer-field/key "III" "$y = 4 - x$"] [:db/cas 17592186055501 :question/problem "Which of the numbers below belong to  ${R}_{0}^{-}?$\n- **I.** $-\\pi $\n- **II.** $\\sqrt{3}$\n- **III.** $-\\frac{4}{5}$\n- **IV.** $0$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the numbers below belong to  ${R}_{0}^{-}?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186055501 :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${R}_{0}^{-}$ is the set of all negative real numbers and $0.$\n\n- $-\\pi $ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $\\sqrt{3}$ is a positive number, so it does *not* belong to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\frac{4}{5}$ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $0$ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"." "Remember that ${R}_{0}^{-}$ is the set of all negative real numbers and $0.$\n\n- $-\\pi $ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $\\sqrt{3}$ is a positive number, so it does *not* belong to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\frac{4}{5}$ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $0$ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $-\\pi $; $-\\frac{4}{5}$; “The number $0$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186461000 :answer-field/key "I" "$-\\pi $"] [:db/cas 17592186461001 :answer-field/key "II" "$\\sqrt{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186461002 :answer-field/key "III" "$-\\frac{4}{5}$"] [:db/cas 17592186461003 :answer-field/key "IV" "The number $0$"] [:db/cas 17592186060020 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(M)=10$\n- **II.** $det(M)=-5$\n- **III.** $M$ is a singular matrix\n\n \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060020 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 4\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{1}{3}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}(12-15)-0+\\frac{1}{3}(0+9\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{5}+3\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 4\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{1}{3}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}(12-15)-0+\\frac{1}{3}(0+9\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{5}+3\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$M$ is a singular matrix” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186440096 :answer-field/key "I" "$det(M)=10$"] [:db/cas 17592186440097 :answer-field/key "II" "$det(M)=-5$"] [:db/cas 17592186440098 :answer-field/key "III" "$M$ is a singular matrix"] [:db/cas 17592186060113 :question/problem "$\\,$ **If** $10\\ge 1,$ **then** $3\\in R.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n- **I.** If $10<1,$ then $3\\notin R.$\n- **II.** If $3\\notin R,$ then $10<1.$\n- **III.** If $3\\in R,$ then $10\\ge 1.$\n\nCompare the logical forms for arbitrary truth values of the antecedent and consequent.\n\nSelect all that apply." "$\\,$ **If** $10\\ge 1,$ **then** $3\\in R.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n\nCompare the logical forms for arbitrary truth values of the antecedent and consequent.\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186060113 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other, but not to the original statement.\n\n![](images/d9/d946d2a81d46cbde934ebbab54f9b69b514092e02da4f8a3b79768f218929449.png)\n\nSo, we are looking for the contrapositive of the given statement.\n\n- Statement I is the inverse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement II is the contrapositive. $\\,✓$\n\n- Statement III is the converse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other, but not to the original statement.\n\n![](images/d9/d946d2a81d46cbde934ebbab54f9b69b514092e02da4f8a3b79768f218929449.png)\n\nSo, we are looking for the contrapositive of the given statement.\n\n- Statement “If $10<1,$ then $3\\notin R.$” is the inverse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement “If $3\\notin R,$ then $10<1.$” is the contrapositive. $\\,✓$\n\n- Statement “If $3\\in R,$ then $10\\ge 1.$” is the converse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $3\\notin R,$ then $10<1.$” only"] [:db/cas 17592186477559 :answer-field/key "I" "If $10<1,$ then $3\\notin R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186477560 :answer-field/key "II" "If $3\\notin R,$ then $10<1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186477561 :answer-field/key "III" "If $3\\in R,$ then $10\\ge 1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186060143 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $P⇒Q≡¬P∨Q$\n- **II.** $¬(P⇒Q)≡P∧¬Q$\n- **III.** $¬(P⇒Q)≡P∨¬Q$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186060143 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q.$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nP⇒Q≡¬P∨Q\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(P⇒Q)≡¬(¬P∨Q).\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(¬P∨Q)≡¬(¬P)∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nP∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(P⇒Q)$ is equivalent to $P∧¬Q.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement $P⇒Q≡¬P∨Q$ is true. Indeed, $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q.$\n\n- Statement $¬(P⇒Q)≡P∧¬Q$ is true, while statement $¬(P⇒Q)≡P∨¬Q$ is false. From part $P⇒Q≡¬P∨Q$, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nP⇒Q≡¬P∨Q\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(P⇒Q)≡¬(¬P∨Q).\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(¬P∨Q)≡¬(¬P)∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nP∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(P⇒Q)$ is equivalent to $P∧¬Q.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $P⇒Q≡¬P∨Q$; $¬(P⇒Q)≡P∧¬Q$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186460719 :answer-field/key "I" "$P⇒Q≡¬P∨Q$"] [:db/cas 17592186460720 :answer-field/key "II" "$¬(P⇒Q)≡P∧¬Q$"] [:db/cas 17592186460721 :answer-field/key "III" "$¬(P⇒Q)≡P∨¬Q$"] [:db/cas 17592186060220 :question/problem "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}?$\n- **I.** $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$\n- **II.** $\\frac{10\\pi }{3}$\n- **III.** $-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186060220 :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{10\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than $\\frac{10\\pi }{3}.$ So, we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{14\\pi }{3}$\n\nOf the given angles, all three are coterminal with $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nSo the correct answer is I, II, and III." "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{10\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than $\\frac{10\\pi }{3}.$ So, we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{14\\pi }{3}$\n\nOf the given angles, all three are coterminal with $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nSo the correct answer is $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$, $\\frac{10\\pi }{3}$, and $-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}$."] [:db/cas 17592186460738 :answer-field/key "I" "$\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186460739 :answer-field/key "II" "$\\frac{10\\pi }{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186460740 :answer-field/key "III" "$-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186060446 :question/problem "Given that the determinant of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ x & 2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & x + 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nis $8$, find all the possible values of $x$.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $x=-8$\n- **II.** $x=-2$\n- **III.** $x=2$\n- **IV.** $x=5$\n- **V.** $x=6$\n- **VI.** $x=8$" "Given that the determinant of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ x & 2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & x + 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nis $8$, find all the possible values of $x$.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186440363 :answer-field/key "I" "$x=-8$"] [:db/cas 17592186440364 :answer-field/key "II" "$x=-2$"] [:db/cas 17592186440365 :answer-field/key "III" "$x=2$"] [:db/cas 17592186440366 :answer-field/key "IV" "$x=5$"] [:db/cas 17592186440367 :answer-field/key "V" "$x=6$"] [:db/cas 17592186440368 :answer-field/key "VI" "$x=8$"] [:db/cas 17592186060454 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $F$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(F)=0$\n- **II.** $F$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(F)=-2$\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $F$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060454 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(F) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-1(24-30)+2(21-24) \\\\  & =0-1\\cdot (-6)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6-6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(F)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(F) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-1(24-30)+2(21-24) \\\\  & =0-1\\cdot (-6)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6-6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(F)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements $det(F)=0$ and “$F$ is a singular matrix” are true."] [:db/cas 17592186439364 :answer-field/key "I" "$det(F)=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186439365 :answer-field/key "II" "$F$ is a singular matrix"] [:db/cas 17592186439366 :answer-field/key "III" "$det(F)=-2$"] [:db/cas 17592186064512 :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-1, & x<1 \\\\ 2-x, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n\n- **II.** ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$\n\n- **III.** ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=f(1)$\n\n- **IV.** $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-1, & x<1 \\\\ 2-x, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186064512 :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the second branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)=2-(1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(2x-1) \\\\  & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(2-x) \\\\  & =2-(1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1=f(1).$  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since all statements I-III are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$” is true. Using the expression for the second branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)=2-(1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$ is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(2x-1) \\\\  & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(2-x) \\\\  & =2-(1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=f(1)$ is true. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1=f(1).$  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$” is true. Since all statements “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$”; ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$; ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=f(1)$ are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""] [:db/cas 17592186460643 :answer-field/key "I" "$f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$"] [:db/cas 17592186460644 :answer-field/key "II" "${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$"] [:db/cas 17592186460645 :answer-field/key "III" "${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=f(1)$"] [:db/cas 17592186460646 :answer-field/key "IV" "$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$"] [:db/cas 17592186064582 :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-6x+12,$ which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $x=-1$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- **II.** ${f}^{″}(-1)<0$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-1$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-6x+12,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186064582 :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=-1$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-6x+12,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-6=6{x}^{2}-6,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ 6({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 6(x+1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=\\pm 1.$ We're interested in the critical point $x=-1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(-1)=-12<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=-1,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=-1$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=-1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "We need to identify whether $x=-1$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-6x+12,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-6=6{x}^{2}-6,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ 6({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 6(x+1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=\\pm 1.$ We're interested in the critical point $x=-1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(-1)=-12<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=-1,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=-1$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=-1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$x=-1$ is a critical point of $f(x)$”; ${f}^{″}(-1)<0$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186460729 :answer-field/key "I" "$x=-1$ is a critical point of $f(x)$"] [:db/cas 17592186460730 :answer-field/key "II" "${f}^{″}(-1)<0$"] [:db/cas 17592186460731 :answer-field/key "III" "$f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-1$"] [:db/cas 17592186064586 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{5}+5{x}^{3}-90x+108.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(-\\sqrt{3})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{″}(-\\sqrt{3})<0$\n- **II.** $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{5}+5{x}^{3}-90x+108.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(-\\sqrt{3})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186064586 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=-\\sqrt{3}$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{5}+5{x}^{3}-90x+108,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=5{x}^{4}+15{x}^{2}-90.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=20{x}^{3}+30x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\sqrt{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-\\sqrt{3}) & =20{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{3}+30(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =20\\cdot 3(-\\sqrt{3})+30(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =-90\\sqrt{3}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "We're given that $x=-\\sqrt{3}$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{5}+5{x}^{3}-90x+108,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=5{x}^{4}+15{x}^{2}-90.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=20{x}^{3}+30x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\sqrt{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-\\sqrt{3}) & =20{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{3}+30(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =20\\cdot 3(-\\sqrt{3})+30(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =-90\\sqrt{3}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${f}^{″}(-\\sqrt{3})<0$; “$f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$” only"] [:db/cas 17592186449289 :answer-field/key "I" "${f}^{″}(-\\sqrt{3})<0$"] [:db/cas 17592186449290 :answer-field/key "II" "$f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186449291 :answer-field/key "III" "$f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186064612 :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ defined on the closed interval $\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le e,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$\nII. The global minimum value is $e-2$\nIII. The global minimum value is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$\n\nSelect all that apply." "For the function $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ defined on the closed interval $\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le e,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186064612 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{2}{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1-\\frac{2}{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2,$ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},e$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈1.9 \\\\ f(2) & =2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈0.6 \\\\ f(e) & =e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=e-2≈0.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global minimum is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"\n\n![](images/6c/6cc3ee476c8c5dc9e27051c990b184318147caffd2177b760074172fe5c667f6.png)" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{2}{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1-\\frac{2}{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2,$ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},e$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈1.9 \\\\ f(2) & =2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈0.6 \\\\ f(e) & =e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=e-2≈0.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global minimum is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, “The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$”; “The global minimum value is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$” only\n\n![](images/6c/6cc3ee476c8c5dc9e27051c990b184318147caffd2177b760074172fe5c667f6.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186477568 :answer-field/key "I" "The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$"] [:db/cas 17592186477569 :answer-field/key "II" "The global minimum value is $e-2$"] [:db/cas 17592186477570 :answer-field/key "III" "The global minimum value is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$"] [:db/cas 17592186064639 :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- **II.** $x=0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- **III.** ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- **IV.** $x=1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$" "For the function $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186064639 :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12x-12{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12x(1-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12-24x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III and IV are true. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I, II, III, and IV.\"" "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12x-12{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12x(1-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12-24x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements ${f}^{″}(0)>0$ and “$x=0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$” are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements ${f}^{″}(1)<0$ and “$x=1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$” are true. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, ${f}^{″}(0)>0$; “$x=0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$”; ${f}^{″}(1)<0$; “$x=1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$”"] [:db/cas 17592186460741 :answer-field/key "I" "${f}^{″}(0)>0$"] [:db/cas 17592186460742 :answer-field/key "II" "$x=0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$"] [:db/cas 17592186460743 :answer-field/key "III" "${f}^{″}(1)<0$"] [:db/cas 17592186460744 :answer-field/key "IV" "$x=1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$"] [:db/cas 17592186064814 :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5]?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)={x}^{3}$\n\n- **II.** $g(x)=|x|$\n\n- **III.** $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5]?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186064814 :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{3}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=x$ over the given interval. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0$ but this point does not lie inside $(1,5).$\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ is not defined at $x=1$, so it is not continuous at $x=1$, which means it is not continuous on $[1,5].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions I and II satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5].$" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{3}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=x$ over the given interval. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0$ but this point does not lie inside $(1,5).$\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ is not defined at $x=1$, so it is not continuous at $x=1$, which means it is not continuous on $[1,5].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $g(x)=|x|$ satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5].$"] [:db/cas 17592186460899 :answer-field/key "I" "$f(x)={x}^{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186460900 :answer-field/key "II" "$g(x)=|x|$"] [:db/cas 17592186460901 :answer-field/key "III" "$h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$"] [:db/cas 17592186064820 :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n- **I.** $g(x)=|x|$\n- **II.** $s(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- **III.** $h(x)=2x$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186064820 :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ but has a corner at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x=0$, so it is not continuous at $x=0$, which means it is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function III satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ but has a corner at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x=0$, so it is not continuous at $x=0$, which means it is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function $h(x)=2x$ satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$"] [:db/cas 17592186460902 :answer-field/key "I" "$g(x)=|x|$"] [:db/cas 17592186460903 :answer-field/key "II" "$s(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$"] [:db/cas 17592186460904 :answer-field/key "III" "$h(x)=2x$"] [:db/cas 17592186064835 :question/problem "![](images/bb/bb17384f2313820f526f1ba4356f6f3c9e11fbb3791e4f7e2c6044cc604bf662.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=2$\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/bb/bb17384f2313820f526f1ba4356f6f3c9e11fbb3791e4f7e2c6044cc604bf662.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186064835 :question/worked-solution "