ft-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."] [:db/cas 17592186460827 :answer-field/key "I" "The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$"] [:db/cas 17592186460828 :answer-field/key "II" "The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$"] [:db/cas 17592186460829 :answer-field/key "III" "The function is continuous at $x=-1$"] [:db/cas 17592186069366 :question/problem "![](images/8e/8eb53506d9e01d09fe3e072a2c6ed1ade05e1cc185693bdd66b34bdf75797ad9.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite\n- **II.** ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$ is finite\n- **III.** $f(x)$  has a discontinuity at $x=2$" "![](images/8e/8eb53506d9e01d09fe3e072a2c6ed1ade05e1cc185693bdd66b34bdf75797ad9.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above.\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186069366 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function contains a break exactly at $x=2$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper." "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite” is false. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement “${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$ is finite” is true. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$  has a discontinuity at $x=2$” is true. The function contains a break exactly at $x=2$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."] [:db/cas 17592186439189 :answer-field/key "I" "${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite"] [